## 12. Query Performance and Partition Filtering

### Rules

#### 12a — Always filter on partition columns

When reading a partitioned table, include a predicate on its partition columns (`year`, `month`, `day`, or the date column declared as `partitions` in the DAG declaration). Without this, Databricks reads every partition (full table scan).

**Good:**

```sql
SELECT
    id_contract,
    status,
    ts_updated
FROM
    datalake_ebdb_clean.contract
WHERE
    year = YEAR('{load_start_date}')
    AND month = MONTH('{load_start_date}')
    AND day = DAY('{load_start_date}')
```

**Bad:**

```sql
-- No partition filter — reads all partitions
SELECT
    id_contract,
    status,
    ts_updated
FROM
    datalake_ebdb_clean.contract
```

#### 12b — Use incremental load params for large tables

For tables that grow continuously, use the `{load_start_date}` / `{load_end_date}` template params (resolved by the Spark job at runtime) instead of querying all historical data on every run.

**Good:**

```sql
SELECT
    id_event,
    event_type,
    ts_event
FROM
    datalake_amplitude_clean.event
WHERE
    dt_event >= '{load_start_date}'
    AND dt_event < '{load_end_date}'
```

**Bad:**

```sql
-- Full scan every run — never use for large, growing tables
SELECT
    id_event,
    event_type,
    ts_event
FROM
    datalake_amplitude_clean.event
```

#### 12c — Push filters before joins

Apply `WHERE` filters inside a CTE before joining to large tables. Filtering after the join forces Databricks to read and shuffle the full dataset first.

**Good:**

```sql
WITH recent_contracts AS (
    SELECT
        id_contract,
        id_user,
        status
    FROM
        datalake_ebdb_clean.contract
    WHERE
        dt_signed >= '{load_start_date}'
        AND dt_signed < '{load_end_date}'
)
SELECT
    rc.id_contract,
    rc.status,
    du.user_name
FROM
    recent_contracts AS rc
LEFT JOIN
    dw_rent.dim_user AS du
        ON rc.id_user = du.id_user
```

**Bad:**

```sql
-- Filter applied after join — full table scan on both sides
SELECT
    c.id_contract,
    c.status,
    du.user_name
FROM
    datalake_ebdb_clean.contract AS c
LEFT JOIN
    dw_rent.dim_user AS du
        ON c.id_user = du.id_user
WHERE
    c.dt_signed >= '{load_start_date}'
    AND c.dt_signed < '{load_end_date}'
```

#### 12d — Align predicates with Z-order columns

When a table declares `z_order_by` columns in its DAG declaration, include those columns in `WHERE` or `JOIN` predicates whenever the query logic allows it. Z-ordering clusters data by those columns on disk; predicates on those columns skip data files via data skipping statistics.

```sql
-- Z-ordered by id_user and dt_event — both columns used in the WHERE clause
SELECT
    id_event,
    event_type,
    ts_event
FROM
    datalake_amplitude_clean.event
WHERE
    id_user = '{user_id}'
    AND dt_event >= '{load_start_date}'
    AND dt_event < '{load_end_date}'
```

